<h2>The allegations and the procedural history</h2>
<p>The FIR, registered on 29 July 2026, alleged that the applicant contacted the complainant through Instagram in late 2025, repeatedly promised marriage, and thereafter committed forcible intercourse on three identified dates — 21 December 2025, 8 March 2026 and 3 June 2026 — with a further attempt in April 2026 and threats of self-harm when confronted. Section 64(2)(m) BNS was added in the course of investigation.</p>
<p>The procedural journey was itself eventful. The applicant was detained in Patna, where the Chief Judicial Magistrate declined transit remand and released him on a bond. The Additional Sessions Judge at Saket dismissed his anticipatory bail plea on 10 August 2026. The High Court granted interim protection on 17 August 2026, and the applicant joined the investigation on several dates. When the matter came on for final hearing, the State pointed to call detail and location records, hotel records, the disappearance of the applicant's earlier phone, and the deletion of his Instagram correspondence.</p>

<h2>What the court held</h2>
<div class="flow">
<div class="fstep"><strong>The charge is rape, not a Section 69 case.</strong> The allegation was of forcible intercourse on specific occasions. Section 69 BNS is residual, applying only where the act does not amount to rape; it cannot reframe an allegation of force into one of mere broken promise.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Consent is act-specific.</strong> The court observed that consent to a relationship is not tantamount to consent to each act within it. A continuing acquaintance, even an intimate one, does not immunise particular acts alleged to be forcible.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Joining investigation is not enough.</strong> Relying on the Supreme Court's decisions in Sumitha Pradeep and P. Krishna Mohan Reddy, the court held custodial interrogation may be necessary notwithstanding cooperation, particularly where devices are missing and digital records stand deleted.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Credibility of the defence version.</strong> The applicant's explanation that his phone was snatched at knife-point found no supporting police complaint on record; the court found the account lacking in credibility at this stage and a matter needing investigation.</div>
</div>
<div class="note">
<p>The court vacated the interim protection, holding that such protection is an interim arrangement pending the State's reply and not an adjudicated grant, and that a material change in circumstances — the status report, electronic records and inconsistencies in the applicant's account — justified withdrawing it. The applicant's defence, the court said, is one to be tested at trial; he remains free to surrender and seek regular bail.</p>
</div>

<h2>Where this fits in anticipatory bail law</h2>
<p>Anticipatory bail under Section 482 of the Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita, 2023 — the successor to Section 438 CrPC — protects the citizen against arrest on frivolous or motivated accusations. But in offences of sexual violence, the courts have consistently required something exceptional before granting pre-arrest protection: a prima facie case of false implication apparent from the record, not merely a competing narrative. This order illustrates the analysis. The court did not decide whom to believe; it asked whether the material disclosed a case needing custodial investigation, and found that it did — outstanding device recoveries, deleted correspondence and an account of events that could not be verified without confrontation.</p>
<p>The order also continues a visible trend in BNS-era jurisprudence of policing the boundary between Section 64 and Section 69. Accused persons have sought to characterise rape allegations as false-promise cases attracting the lighter residual provision; complainants have feared the new section would dilute rape prosecutions. The Delhi High Court's approach keeps the provisions in their lanes: where force is alleged on identified occasions, the case proceeds as rape, and the residual section does not enter the analysis.</p>

<h2>Practical guidance</h2>
<div class="check">
<p>For applicants: interim protection is not a destination. Use the protected period to genuinely assist the investigation, and treat outstanding recoveries — especially devices and digital records — as the single biggest obstacle to final relief.</p>
<p>Explanations for missing evidence must be contemporaneously documented; an unreported phone-snatching will carry little weight months later.</p>
<p>For complainants and the prosecution: specific dates, places and electronic corroboration materially strengthen opposition to pre-arrest bail, as this order shows.</p>
</div>
